    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Single-reed woodwinds: reed vibrates against mouthpiece. Despite 'woodwind' name, modern bodies are often plastic or metal."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "clarinet",
      "title": "Clarinet",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Cylinder bore; cylindrical bore overblows at twelfth. B♭ clarinet most common. Wide dynamic range. Mozart and Brahms clarinet quintets. Klezmer and jazz traditions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "saxophone",
      "title": "Saxophone",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Adolphe Sax, 1840s Belgium. Conical bore overblows at octave. Family from soprano to baritone. Central to jazz — Charlie Parker (alto), John Coltrane (tenor)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "double-reeds",
      "title": "Double reeds",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Oboe and bassoon use two reeds bound together. Distinctive nasal oboe tone; bassoon's wide range in orchestra."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
